`int A[1024][1024];` (4-byte `int`) is stored in **row-major** order starting on a page boundary. Pages are **4 KB**, the TLB has **64** entries, is fully associative with **LRU** replacement, and starts empty. The page table is single-level. TLB lookup is **10 ns** (spent on every reference) and memory access is **100 ns**. All pages of A are resident, there is no data cache, and only the references to A count.

```c
// Loop R (row-wise)
for (i = 0; i < 1024; i++)
    for (j = 0; j < 1024; j++)
        sum += A[i][j];

// Loop C (column-wise)
for (j = 0; j < 1024; j++)
    for (i = 0; i < 1024; i++)
        sum += A[i][j];
```

What is (total memory-access time of loop C) ÷ (total memory-access time of loop R), approximately?

`int a[81920];` (4-byte `int`) starts on a page boundary. Pages are **4 KB**, the TLB is fully associative with **64** entries, **LRU** replacement, and starts empty. Paging is **two-level** with both levels in memory. TLB lookup = **10 ns** (spent on every reference), memory access = **100 ns**. All pages are resident; there is no data cache; only the references to `a` count.

```c
for (r = 0; r < 10; r++) {
    if (r % 2 == 0)
        for (i = 0; i < 81920; i += 512) sum += a[i];        // forward
    else
        for (i = 81920 - 512; i >= 0; i -= 512) sum += a[i];  // backward
}
```

What is the total memory-access time for the references to `a`, in **microseconds**?
